WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.160
Recall our original four by
four circulant Hadamard matrix.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:07.080
Its first row is negative
one, one, one, one.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:11.300
Each following row is a right
cyclic shift. Our goal is to

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:15.420
replace the matrix by one algebraic
object that remembers all those shifts.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:19.060
Use the entries as
polynomial coefficients.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.680
We get h of X equals negative one plus

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.240
X plus X squared plus X cubed.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:31.860
Work with the rule X to the fourth
equals one, so the powers wrap around.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:35.880
In dimension n, this is the integer
group ring of the cyclic group:

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:39.840
integer polynomials modulo
X to the n minus one.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:45.000
Its unique representative of degree less
than n records the row unambiguously.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.180
Now multiply h by X.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.880
The last term becomes X to
the fourth, which is one.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:55.045
The coefficients become
one, negative one, one, one.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:01:00.680
That is exactly a right cyclic shift.
Multiply by X again for the next row.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:05.200
Row j corresponds to X to
the j minus one times h.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.340
After n shifts, the cycle returns to h.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.960
To encode dot products we
need another operation: star.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.560
Replace X by X inverse.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.980
This fixes the constant coefficient and
reverses the other cyclic positions.

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:25.160
With generic coefficients, a,
b, c, d becomes a, d, c, b.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:29.400
Our actual row is unchanged because
its final three entries agree.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.740
Thus, for this example, h star equals h.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:37.780
Star reverses positions; it does
not negate the coefficients.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:42.020
Multiply h by h star in this group ring.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:47.780
The coefficient at each power X to the a
is the dot product of the first row with

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:51.940
its a step right shift. At the constant
term we compare the row with itself:

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:57.760
four squares of signs give four. At X
we get negative one, negative one, one,

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:03.440
one, whose sum is zero. The two step and
three step dot products are also zero.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.960
So every nonconstant coefficient vanishes.
The whole product is simply four.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:14.820
One equation has encoded every
orthogonality condition of our example.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.360
The same dictionary
works in every dimension,

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.840
provided all n coefficients
are positive or negative one.

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:26.760
The matrix equation, H times H
transpose equals n times the identity,

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:31.060
is equivalent to h times h star
equals n in the group ring.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.860
Forward, orthogonality makes the
nonconstant coefficients zero,

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:38.740
while the signs give constant
coefficient n. Conversely,

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:43.920
the polynomial identity makes the first
row orthogonal to every nontrivial shift.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.400
Shift any pair of rows back together:
their dot product becomes one of those

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.200
same correlations. Every distinct
pair is therefore orthogonal.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.320
The sign hypothesis is essential.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:03:00.760
In dimension four, the constant polynomial
two also has product four with its star,

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:05.440
but its coefficient row
is two, zero, zero, zero.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:09.100
It gives twice the identity
matrix, not a Hadamard matrix.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:11.360
With the sign hypothesis in place,

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:15.780
the dictionary is exact. Explore the
five smaller nodes for its ingredients.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:19.400
Next we use the identity
to restrict the order n.
